{"name":"Astroturf","hex":"#67a159","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Astroturf","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with an artificial grasslike material.","To fabricate an impression of political support for an agenda."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a group created by a larger organisation (especially a corporation or political party), but presenting itself as a grassroots organisation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Astroturf"}],"entries":[{"title":"AstroTurf","blocks":[{"text":"AstroTurf is an American subsidiary of SportGroup that produces artificial turf for playing surfaces in sports. The original AstroTurf product was a short-pile synthetic turf invented in 1965 by Monsanto. Since the early 2000s, AstroTurf has marketed taller pile systems that use infill materials to better replicate natural turf. In 2016, AstroTurf became a subsidiary of German-based SportGroup, a family of sports surfacing companies, which itself is owned by the investment firm Equistone Partners Europe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Artificial_turf_at_a_stadium.jpg/330px-Artificial_turf_at_a_stadium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AstroTurf","wikidata":"Q4593385"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/James_russell_washington_state.jpg/500px-James_russell_washington_state.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/James_russell_washington_state.jpg/1280px-James_russell_washington_state.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/James_russell_washington_state.jpg/500px-James_russell_washington_state.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","a